::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which number is greatest? {
~-9 #This is the farthest left value, so it is the least, not the greatest.
~-2 #This is greater than -9 but still less than any positive number.
=4 #Correct. Positive 4 is to the right of the negative values and zero.
~0 #Zero is greater than negative numbers but less than 4.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which comparison is true? {
=-7 &lt; -2 #Correct. -7 is farther left on the number line than -2.
~-2 &lt; -7 #This reverses the order. -2 is farther right, so it is greater than -7.
~-7 &gt; 0 #A negative number is less than zero.
~0 &lt; -2 #Zero is greater than any negative number.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the opposite of -11? {
~-11 #This is the original number, not its opposite.
=11 #Correct. Opposites are the same distance from zero on opposite sides of the number line.
~0 #Zero is not the opposite of -11.
~-<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>11</sub> #This is a reciprocal-like error, not the opposite.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate |-13|. {
~-13 #Absolute value is distance from zero, so it cannot be negative.
=13 #Correct. -13 is 13 units from zero.
~0 #The number is not zero units from zero.
~26 #This doubles the distance instead of finding the distance from zero.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Order from least to greatest: -3, 5, -8, 0. {
~-3, -8, 0, 5 #This puts -3 before -8, but -8 is farther left and is smaller.
=-8, -3, 0, 5 #Correct. From left to right on the number line, the order is -8, -3, 0, 5.
~5, 0, -3, -8 #This orders from greatest to least.
~-8, 0, -3, 5 #This places 0 before -3, but 0 is greater than -3.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -9 + 4. {
~-13 #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The signs are different, so subtract the absolute values.
=-5 #Correct. The signs are different, so 9 - 4 = 5, and the negative number has the larger absolute value.
~5 #This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~13 #This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -6 + (-8). {
=-14 #Correct. Same signs mean add the absolute values and keep the negative sign.
~-2 #This subtracts the absolute values, but the signs are the same.
~2 #This subtracts and uses a positive sign, which does not match adding two negatives.
~14 #This adds the absolute values but uses the wrong sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 12 + (-15). {
~-27 #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. Different signs require subtraction.
=-3 #Correct. The signs are different, so 15 - 12 = 3, and the negative value has the larger absolute value.
~3 #This subtracts correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~27 #This adds the absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 7 - 11. {
~-18 #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign. The expression is subtraction.
=-4 #Correct. 7 - 11 means move 11 units left from 7, ending at -4.
~4 #This reverses the subtraction.
~18 #This adds the numbers instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 8 - (-5). {
~3 #This treats subtracting a negative as ordinary subtraction.
=13 #Correct. Subtracting a negative means adding the opposite: 8 + 5 = 13.
~-13 #This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~-3 #This subtracts and then uses a negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -4 - 9. {
=-13 #Correct. -4 - 9 means move 9 more units left from -4.
~-5 #This subtracts the absolute values instead of moving left.
~5 #This ignores the negative direction.
~13 #This uses the right distance but the wrong sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -3 - (-10). {
~-13 #This treats both values as negatives being added.
=7 #Correct. -3 - (-10) = -3 + 10 = 7.
~-7 #This uses the correct distance but wrong direction.
~13 #This adds absolute values instead of adding the opposite.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (-5)(6). {
~-11 #This adds the numbers instead of multiplying them.
=-30 #Correct. Different signs make a negative product, and 5 × 6 = 30.
~30 #This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~11 #This adds absolute values and uses a positive sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (-7)(-2). {
~-14 #This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive product.
~-9 #This adds the values instead of multiplying.
=14 #Correct. A negative times a negative is positive, and 7 × 2 = 14.
~9 #This adds absolute values instead of multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -24 ÷ 6. {
=-4 #Correct. Different signs make a negative quotient, and 24 ÷ 6 = 4.
~4 #This divides correctly but uses the wrong sign.
~-18 #This subtracts instead of dividing.
~30 #This adds absolute values instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -36 ÷ (-9). {
~-4 #This divides correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive quotient.
=4 #Correct. A negative divided by a negative is positive, and 36 ÷ 9 = 4.
~-27 #This subtracts instead of dividing.
~45 #This adds absolute values instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a positive result? {
~(-3)(5) #Different signs make a negative product.
=(-3)(-5) #Correct. Same signs make a positive product.
~-3 - 5 #This moves farther left and gives a negative result.
~-3 + (-5) #Adding two negatives gives a negative result.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression has a negative result? {
=(-8)(3) #Correct. Different signs make a negative product.
~(-8)(-3) #Same signs make a positive product.
~8 + 3 #Adding two positives gives a positive result.
~8 - (-3) #Subtracting a negative means adding, so the result is positive.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. {
~<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> #This adds numerators and denominators, which is not how fractions are added.
=<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #Correct. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, and <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>.
~<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> #This multiplies denominators instead of finding a common denominator.
~<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #This subtracts instead of adding.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. {
=-<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> #Correct. -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, which equals -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>.
~-1 #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign.
~<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> #This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign.
~-<sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> #This equals -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>, but it is not written in simplest form.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> - <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>. {
~1 #This subtracts in the wrong order.
=-1 #Correct. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> - <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = -<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = -1.
~<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>0</sub> #This subtracts denominators, which is not valid.
~-<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> #This subtracts denominators incorrectly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> × 10. {
~-<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub> #This multiplies the denominator by 10 instead of multiplying the value.
=-4 #Correct. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> of 10 is 4, and the negative sign makes the result -4.
~4 #This finds the size but loses the negative sign.
~-12 #This subtracts instead of multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A temperature changes from -3°F to 5°F. What is the change in temperature? {
~-8°F #This subtracts in the wrong direction.
=8°F #Correct. Moving from -3 to 5 is an increase of 8 degrees.
~2°F #This adds -3 and 5 instead of finding the change.
~-2°F #This adds the values and keeps a negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says -10 is greater than -4 because 10 is greater than 4. What is the best correction? {
~-10 is greater because it has the larger absolute value. #This repeats the misconception. For negative numbers, larger absolute value means farther left.
=-10 is less because it is farther left on the number line. #Correct. Number line position determines which signed number is greater or less.
~-10 and -4 are equal because both are negative. #Negative numbers can have different values.
~-4 is less because 4 is smaller than 10. #This ignores the negative signs and number line positions.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student evaluates 6 - (-2) as 4. What is the best correction? {
~The answer is 4 because subtracting always makes a number smaller. #This is the misconception. Subtracting a negative increases the value.
=6 - (-2) = 6 + 2 = 8 because subtracting a negative means adding the opposite. #Correct. The opposite of -2 is 2, so the expression becomes 6 + 2.
~6 - (-2) = -8 because two negative signs make the answer negative. #Two signs change the operation here; they do not make the answer negative.
~6 - (-2) = 12 because the signs mean multiply. #The expression is subtraction, not multiplication.
}
